  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1241 AM CDT Wed Aug 19 2026\n\nSurface analysis this evening places a quasi-stationary front\nfrom the Central Plains into portions of the Lower Mississippi\nValley with the front effectively just to the south of the\nforecast area. Convection Tuesday afternoon was limited to a\nsingle strong thunderstorm over northeast Mississippi. Otherwise,\ndry conditions have persisted across the Mid-South into the\nevening with temperatures in the 80s to around 90 degrees with\nsurface dewpoints in the 70s.\n\nThe aforementioned quasi-stationary boundary will begin to lift\nback north as a warm front overnight into Wednesday. Short-term\nmodels including CAMs indicate convective development over\neastern Kansas and western Missouri overnight gradually spreading\nsoutheast and weakening as it approaches the Mid-South by sunrise\nWednesday. This activity may leave some remnant outflow\nboundaries up near the MO/KY border with mainly rain free\nconditions occurring during the day, especially along and south\nof I-40.\n\nTemperatures across the Mid-South will rise back into the mid 90s\nto lower 100s again on Wednesday. These very hot temperatures\ncombined with dewpoints in the lower to middle 70s will produce\nheat index values between 105 to 112 degrees across the Mid-\nSouth. The Extreme Heat Warning was expanded north to Jonesboro\nand up to portions of the Memphis metro area as short-term\nguidance indicates the potential for moisture to pool in this\nregion. A Heat Advisory was also expanded up to the Missouri\nborder where heat index values may reach Advisory criteria.\nOtherwise, no other modifications were made to the Advisory and\nWarnings in effect.\n\nLatest guidance indicates showers and thunderstorms will develop\nWednesday afternoon over southern Missouri and slowly spread\nsoutheast into the Mid-South Wednesday night into early Thursday\nmorning. Considerable uncertainty remains between guidance with\nthe latest 00Z NAM keeping most of the activity north of the Mid-\nSouth with other model solutions suggesting development after 06Z\nThursday. Moderately steep mid-level lapse rates, MLCAPE values\nbetween 1500-2500 J/kg, and precipitable water values around\n2 inches support the potential for severe thunderstorms. Model\nsoundings show some favorable storm relative helicities with\nclockwise curved hodographs but also feature parcels becoming\nelevated throughout the night with a capping inversion below\n850 mb. Thus, confidence in any tornado development remains very\nlow at best. Damaging winds will be the primary threat, with a\nsecondary threat of localized heavy rainfall and perhaps some\nmarginally severe hail.\n\nShower and thunderstorm chances will continue Thursday into\nThursday night as a mid-level trough pushes into the eastern\nU.S., allowing a cold front to push through the Mid-South. This\nfront will bring some relief with cooler temperatures, especially\nover portions of West Tennessee into the upcoming weekend.",
